 of Y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049633 #edb/ref 1033 3689 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049633 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "070195a8-e50d-5739-83b7-3404856d4300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050616 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050616 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186051000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050616 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050616 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050616 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050616 #edb/ref 1031 "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Ellipses" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050616 #edb/ref 1033 3690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050616 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5903ee12-0487-5a55-b940-9a4cc808313e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050666 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050666 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050666 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050666 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050666 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050666 #edb/ref 1031 "Sums of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y Over Parametric Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050666 #edb/ref 1033 3705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050666 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5d68aacb-ca27-5530-ae69-05289931b444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186179746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1031 "Calculating Flux in Two-Dimensional Vector Fields" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1033 3716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050793 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6768fffe-a644-5bd7-b35c-6b2cc2563786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179529 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179529 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7a/7a6e67435aab40d5bf3630c66f79241008357e1549b1c77b4c0bdfea2b453883.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\dfrac{y\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x\\,\\mathrm{d}y}{x^2+y^2},$ where $C$ is a part of the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $\\left(0,2\\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cadcaa9-876e-56cb-b8b3-17695827c4f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179529 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {x}^{′}-x\\cdot {y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}},dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179530 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179530 #edb/ref 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=x^2$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ evaluate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + (x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a63ca22-0b1a-5584-8b43-c80457016cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179530 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179590 #edb/ref 1069 "e-9515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179590 #edb/ref 1071 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1, 1),$ calculate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179590 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d13f401-5e07-5b07-8916-df2b0b6b8f74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179590 #edb/ref 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a} = 0\\,\\mathbf{i} + 0\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = 1\\,\\mathbf{i} + 1\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ So, we have that $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot t\\cdot 1+t\\cdot t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186387547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179673 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186